1. **Hash the tail of the payload (reqID), not the prefix.**  
   Use the last 4 bytes, which include part of the changing `reqID`, to get **per‑packet variation even on a single UDPConn**:

   ```c
   __u32 h = reuse->hash;

   void *data = (void *)(long)reuse->data;
   void *data_end = (void *)(long)reuse->data_end;

   if (data && data_end && data < data_end) {
       __u64 len = (char *)data_end - (char *)data;
       if (len >= 4) {
           unsigned char *tail = (unsigned char *)data_end - 4;
           __u32 t = ((__u32)tail[0] << 24) |
                     ((__u32)tail[1] << 16) |
                     ((__u32)tail[2] << 8)  |
                     ((__u32)tail[3]);

           // simple mix; still O(1) and stateless
           h ^= t;
           h *= 16777619u;
           h += 2166136261u;
       }
   }

   __u32 slot = h % active;
   bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot, 0);
   ```

   This keeps the program stateless and verifier‑safe, but now **every request gets a different hash**, so all workers can be used even with a single flow.

2. **Optionally go even simpler: drop `reuse->hash` entirely.**  
   If you want to maximize independence from the number of flows, you can base `h` purely on the tail:

   ```c
   __u32 h = 0x811c9dc5u; // FNV offset basis

   // same tail extraction as above
   if (len >= 4) {
       __u32 t = ...;
       h ^= t;
       h *= 16777619u;
   }

   if (h == 0) h = reuse->hash; // fallback in pathological tiny packets

   __u32 slot = h % active;
   ```

   That makes selection depend **only on reqID/payload**, so you’re completely insensitive to how many client UDPConns exist.

3. **After this change, verify basic utilization and service times.**  
   Using server logs at 50k–80k:
   - Confirm all 6 workers see traffic (GET/SCAN counts > 0 and reasonably balanced).
   - Check `avg_get_us ≈ 10` and `avg_scan_us ≈ 2000`. If those drift, something else is wrong; if they’re good but p99 is high, then the remaining issue is pure queueing and we can consider further affinity tweaks next.

These steps should restore true per‑packet spreading (reqID‑based), remove flow‑count sensitivity, and give a cleaner baseline to evaluate queueing and p99 behavior.

